## Where I believe the reviewer is wrong (NOT applied)
1. et-a #17 / et-b #5 — "Estonian thousands take a space, not a period." Correct as general Estonian
   orthography, but the campaign rule (R14) and ACCEPTED-ROUND fix the et corpus on dot-thousands
   because the translation checker's number gate cannot parse "500 000". The reviewer repeated this
   row from round 4 without registering the standing decision; it should be closed as a known
   deviation rather than re-filed each round.
2. et-a #59 — "TWD 1.57M" → "1,57 miljonit TWD". The bio figure is pinned byte-identical by a unit
   test (GUIDANCE_BIO_PINNED_TERMS) and ACCEPTED-ROUND names it explicitly as a skip. Same comment:
   this is a standing decision, not a defect.
3. et-a #40 / et-b #7 — the zero-width-space layout and the missing "##" in 003 from Q16 on are not
   translation defects: they are faithfully copied from the Korean source, and the checker pins both
   the block count and the ZWSP count against it. Repairing this needs a source-side decision
   (003, 005, 006, 008, 009, 013, 015 in src/content/columns), which no locale fixer can take.
4. et-b #18 and #6 suggested wording ("25. juunil 2026", "Alates 1. juulist 2026") is the right
   Estonian but breaks the checker, which knows only nominative Estonian month names. The defect is
   real and was fixed with checker-safe phrasing instead ("seisuga 25. juuni 2026", "Alates
   kuupäevast 1. juuli 2026"). If the campaign wants the natural inflected forms, the fix belongs in
   scripts/check-column-translation.mjs (DATE_MONTH_NAMES for et), not in the content.
5. et-b #30 asked to delete the sentence "Tasub see eelnevalt kontrollida." It is its own body block
   and block counts are pinned, so deletion is not available; the redundancy was removed by moving
   the closing sentence into that block instead. Same for et-a #36 ("sisuks.").
